      "title": "9.5 Ionization constants of acids (Ka)",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "b07b1f6c-f5a4-509d-ae86-73be5bffb0d4",
          "pdf_from": 195,
          "pdf_to": 196,
          "note": "Topic 9.5 with Table 9.3, printed pages 195-196."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Acids and bases, when dissolved in water, may or may not be completely dissociated. Many acids are weak electrolytes and ionize to an extent which is much less than 100%. The value of Ka called the dissociation constant of an acid, is the quantitative measure of the strength of the acid. Suppose we have an acid HA dissolved in water,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\ce{HA + H2O <=> H3O+ + A-}",
            "text_alt": "HA + H₂O ⇌ H₃O⁺ + A⁻"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Kc for the reversible reaction will be written as follows:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\begin{aligned} K_c &= \\frac{[\\ce{H3O+}]\\,[\\ce{A-}]}{[\\ce{HA}]\\,[\\ce{H2O}]} \\\\ K_c[\\ce{H2O}] &= \\frac{[\\ce{H3O+}]\\,[\\ce{A-}]}{[\\ce{HA}]} \\end{aligned}",
            "text_alt": "Kc = [H₃O⁺][A⁻] / [HA][H₂O]; Kc[H₂O] = [H₃O⁺][A⁻] / [HA]"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Let}\\quad K_c[\\ce{H2O}] = K_a",
            "text_alt": "Let Kc[H₂O] = Ka"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\text{Hence}\\quad K_a = \\frac{[\\ce{H3O+}]\\,[\\ce{A-}]}{[\\ce{HA}]}",
            "text_alt": "Hence Ka = [H₃O⁺][A⁻] / [HA]"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "This equation can be used to calculate Ka for any acidic solution if we know the pH or [H⁺] of that solution and the initial concentration of the dissolved acid [HA]. This can also be used to calculate the equilibrium concentration of H₃O⁺ and A⁻ produced if we know the initial concentration of acid HA and its Ka value."
          },
          {
            "type": "list",
            "ordered": false,
            "items": [
              "When Ka < 10⁻³ acid is weak.",
              "Ka = 1 to 10⁻³ acid is moderately strong.",
              "Ka > 1 acid is strong."
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The values of Ka for some acids are given in Table 9.3."
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Table 9.3 The values of Ka for some acids",
            "header": [
              "Acid",
              "Dissociation",
              "Ka",
              "Relative strength"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "HCl",
                "HCl ⇌ H⁺ + Cl⁻",
                "very large (10⁻²)",
                "Very strong"
              ],
              [
                "HNO₃",
                "HNO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + NO₃⁻",
                "very large (10⁻⁵)",
                "Very strong"
              ],
              [
